---
// What an engineer asks before installing a tool that touches production,
// answered the way a colleague would: plainly, with the limits included.
// Every answer is checked against the code; change one only with it.
// "Why not k9s?" is checked against the k9s README, k9scli.io and its latest
// release (v0.51.0, June 2026); recheck it when k9s ships. Its wins stay in:
// they're what make the Speed section's numbers believable. k9s's Ctrl-K
// kill has no confirmation, and it lists every resource type by default
// where safina shows six groups.

// The real `history` block of ~/.safina/config.yaml, with its defaults
// (internal/history/store.go): config shown as it is, which an engineer
// trusts more than a sentence about it. Kept to 42 columns so it fits a phone.
const config = `history:
  actionDays: 90    # writes
  evidenceDays: 14  # logs and events read
  redactPatterns:   # on top of the built-ins
    - 'customer-[0-9]+'
  enabled: true     # false turns it off`;
const lines = config.split("\n").map((line) => {
  const at = line.indexOf("#");
  return at < 0 ? { code: line, note: "" } : { code: line.slice(0, at), note: line.slice(at) };
});
---

<section class="questions" id="questions" aria-labelledby="questions-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <hgroup>
      <p class="section-name">FAQ</p>
      <h2 id="questions-title" class="h2-ref">Fair questions</h2>
    </hgroup>
    <div class="questions__list split">
      <div class="questions__col">
      <div class="question">
        <h3>Why not k9s?</h3>
        <div class="answer">
          They read the same kubeconfig, so you can run both side by side. k9s shows every resource type by default,
          where safina shows six groups and you add the rest. k9s also has a read-only mode, plugins and skins, Windows
          builds and package-manager installs, none of which safina has&nbsp;yet.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="question">
        <h3>What does the model see?</h3>
        <div class="answer">
          Chat sends names, status and numbers for what you’ve selected and your recent Logbook entries for it,
          plus logs when you attach them. Through safina’s MCP server, an agent reads logs on its own, up to 200 lines at a time.
          Neither ever sees a Secret’s data. Apart from the model, safina talks only to your&nbsp;clusters.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="question">
        <h3>Can the model change my cluster?</h3>
        <div class="answer">
          Not through safina. Chat gives it text to read, not keys, and over MCP it can only propose a change,
          which waits until you approve it. If you’ve already let your agent run kubectl without asking, safina
          can’t take that&nbsp;back.
        </div>
      </div>
      </div>
      <div class="questions__col">
      <div class="question">
        <h3>What does it leave on my machine?</h3>
        <div class="answer">
          Everything it keeps is under <code>~/.safina</code>, and the Logbook keeps it as long as its config says:
          <pre class="config"><code>{lines.map((l, i) => <>{l.code}{l.note && <span class="config__note">{l.note}</span>}{i < lines.length - 1 && "\n"}</>)}</code></pre>
          An agent’s MCP sessions and proposals stay until you delete them. The <code>claude</code> CLI keeps chat conversations
          under <code>~/.claude</code>, like its other&nbsp;sessions.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="question">
        <h3>What does it cost?</h3>
        <div class="answer">
          Nothing. It’s MIT-licensed. Chat runs on your own&nbsp;subscription.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="question">
        <h3>What does onboarding the team take?</h3>
        <div class="answer">
          A binary on each laptop. Nobody needs an account, and everyone works through their own kubeconfig.
          Logbooks stay personal; export one for the team’s&nbsp;postmortem.
        </div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .questions {
    padding-top: var(--s5);
    background: var(--abyss);
  }
  .questions h2 {
    max-width: 17ch;
  }
  /* The questions in two columns on the page's two lines, ruled like the
     Also on board list, so the title column doesn't stand empty beside a
     long list. Stacked, one column at the measure. */
  .questions__list {
    margin-top: var(--s3);
    row-gap: var(--s2);

    @media (width <= 1100px) {
      max-width: var(--measure);
    }
  }
  .questions__col {
    display: grid;
    align-content: start;
    gap: var(--s2);
  }
  .question {
    min-width: 0;
    padding-top: var(--s1);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }
  /* Each question is a heading in the title voice, an item's rank
     (--t-h3), so a scan down the list reads the questions alone; the
     answer is in the body ink. */
  .answer {
    margin-top: 0.5em;
    color: var(--spray);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  /* Config as the terminal shows it, on the terminal's own ground like the
     install command; comments in the quiet ink (5.5:1 on that ground). */
  .config {
    margin: var(--s1) 0;
    /* The install command's inset, so the page's two code blocks start
       their text on one line. */
    padding: 14px 22px;
    overflow-x: auto;
    border-radius: var(--radius-frame);
    background: var(--tn-bg);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px rgb(from var(--tn-fg) r g b / 11%);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    /* 42 columns plus padding fit a 390px phone's column at 3.25vw. */
    font-size: min(0.8125rem, 3.25vw);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--tn-fg);
    white-space: pre;
    scrollbar-width: thin;
  }
  .config code {
    font-size: inherit;
  }
  .config__note {
    color: var(--fog);
  }
</style>
